The output isn't really "garbled"; it's just compressed.  The root cause is that curl_easy_impersonate() sets the Accept-Encoding: header to enable compressed transfers, but just setting that header doesn't tell libcurl to decompress data returned from the server.  You have to set the [CURLOPT_ACCEPT_ENCODING](https://curl.se/libcurl/c/CURLOPT_ACCEPT_ENCODING.html) option to enable that.  The quickest fix would be something like this:

```diff
--- lib/easy.c
+++ lib/easy.c
@@ -445,6 +445,9 @@ static CURLcode _do_impersonate(struct Curl_easy *data
     for(i = 0; i < IMPERSONATE_MAX_HEADERS; i++) {
       if(opts->http_headers[i]) {
         headers = curl_slist_append(headers, opts->http_headers[i]);
+        if (strcasecmp(opts->http_headers[i], "accept-encoding:") == ' ') {
+          curl_easy_setopt(data, CURLOPT_ACCEPT_ENCODING, opts->http_headers[i]);
+        }
         if(!headers) {
           return CURLE_OUT_OF_MEMORY;
         }
```

An alternative would be to move the Accept-Encoding: header out of the `struct impersonate_opts`  .http_headers field into its own field, and then in _do_impersonate(), set CURLOPT_ACCEPT_ENCODING if that field is set (similar to how the .cert_compression field ends up setting CURLOPT_SSL_CERT_COMPRESSION).

Once this is fixed, the `--compressed` flag can be removed from the new helper scripts (curl_chrome142 etc) since the `--impersonate nnn` flag will enable that automatically.

> There is also endianness bugs at the C level for big endian on ppc64, so the patch above isnt optional.

